The function has to return a new nested dict equal to d except that the value at the separator-joined path is set, creating missing intermediate dicts and replacing any non-dict found partway along the path with an empty dict, all without mutating d.

The top-level function simply splits the path on sep and hands the keys to a recursive helper, where the work is done:

        new_d = dict(d)
        if not rest:
            new_d[key] = value
        else:
            child = new_d.get(key)
            if not isinstance(child, dict):
                child = {}
            new_d[key] = _set_nested_helper(child, rest, value)

At every level along the path the helper copies the current dict with dict(d) before changing anything, and it only writes into that copy. The last key assigns the value; any earlier key fetches the child, swaps in an empty dict when the child is missing or not a dict, and recurses. Because each dict on the path is replaced by a fresh copy, the originals are never modified, while untouched branches are shared by reference, which is fine since they are not changed.

I traced d = {'a': {'b': 1, 'k': {'z': 0}}, 'q': 3} with path 'a.b.c' and value 7. At 'a' the dict is copied; at 'b' the child is 1, not a dict, so it is replaced by {}; at 'c' the value is set. The result is {'a': {'b': {'c': 7}, 'k': {'z': 0}}, 'q': 3}, and afterwards d still has 1 under 'b'. With a different separator, {'m': {'n': 1}} and 'm/p' using '/' gives {'m': {'n': 1, 'p': 2}} for value 2. No example value appears in the code.

